{-
   A raw pointer at a signature boundary - Analysis-Lens.md §12.3(c).

   # resolver: ownership

   *"An unqualified `%T` at a boundary is refused when the value is frame-rooted."*

   Inside a body nothing is written and the provenance is inferred: `addressOf(x)` keeps `x` alive to
   the pointer's last use, which is the ordinary origin environment and needs no rule of its own. At a
   *signature* the qualifier is the only thing that carries it, so a result or a hand-over whose value
   the environment finds rooted in this frame, written with no qualifier, is where an author is told.

   That it fires at a declaration the author can edit is the point of it firing there at all. Erasing
   it silently is a use-after-free into a reclaimed frame rather than a type error, and a raw pointer
   has nothing for a later pass to walk: copy the address into another local, or hand it to something
   that keeps it, and no rule in the model knows the storage is gone.

   **`Native` code is untouched**, and the last case is what says so: a pointer manufactured from an
   integer, an allocator or foreign code is rooted in no loan at all, so a bare `%T` there stays what
   it is. This is a rule about values the environment can follow, not a migration of every signature
   that names a pointer.

   **The pointer comes from `values`, and `addressOf` answers the same way since D15.** What the
   environment follows is the *pointee* position of the value, and a value has one only where its
   type names an origin: `lib/Core/Array.native.yana` writes `values(self: source'[a]) -> %source'a`
   and `lib/Native/Pointer.yana` now writes `addressOf(it: source'a) -> %source'a`. It used to write
   `it: a`, and a pointer taken that way out of this frame's own storage carried a loan in the body
   and nothing in its type, so every case below written that way was silently accepted - a recorded
   hole, closed. The cases stay written through `values` because that is what they were pinned at;
   `let &v = 7 :: I32; return addressOf(v)` is refused with the same sentence.

   Body errors, all of them - the placement of `yield'` itself is a declaration error and is in
   `lens/Reject.Handover.yana`, because a file whose declarations failed has no bodies walked.
-}

import Native

-- A result rooted in this frame's own storage. The address outlives `buf`, and the signature says
-- nothing that would let a caller know it.
fn leaks() -> %I32:
    let &buf = [7] :: [I32 *1]
    return values(buf)

-- The same one element along, which is the shape that says the arithmetic carries the provenance:
-- the offset pointer is still rooted in `buf` and is still refused.
fn leaksElement() -> %I32:
    let &buf = [1, 2, 3] :: [I32 *3]
    return values(buf) + 1

{-
   And a **hand-over**, which is the other half of §12.3(c)'s sentence and needs a rule of its own.

   A hand-over's root always states something - `Origin::CallBounded` is the default, and is what
   lets a lens yield a view of its own frame at all - so what is missing here is a statement about
   the position *inside* the pointer. Without one, the continuation may copy the address into
   storage that outlives the call: a raw pointer is trivially copyable, so the copy is an owned
   scalar rooted in nothing and no later walk has a term to object with.

   `lens/Handover.yana` is the same declaration written `-> %yield'I32`, which is what the message
   names.
-}
lens fn hands() -> %I32:
    let &cell = [3] :: [I32 *1]
    yield values(cell)

{-
   And the same through a parameter the lens **consumed**.

   `->box` moves the value in, so the slot holding it is the callee's exactly as a `let`'s is and
   dies with the call - which makes this the same mistake `hands` above makes, reached through the
   one root the rule has to look past "is this a parameter" to see. An ordinary `box: [I32 *2]` is
   *not* this case and is why the distinction is the parameter's contract rather than its position:
   this resolver hands a read-only parameter over as a shared call-bounded loan of the caller's
   storage, which outlives the call, and `rooted` below is the same statement written with an origin
   on it.
-}
lens fn handsParameter(->box: [I32 *2]) -> %I32:
    yield values(box)

-- A pointer rooted in a **parameter** the signature published, which is the accepting shape and is
-- here so that the fixture says what the rule is about: the storage is the caller's, the signature
-- says so, and nothing is reported.
fn rooted(xs: source'[I32 *3]) -> %source'I32 = values(xs)

-- A pointer this frame did not derive from any storage. There is no loan for the environment to find,
-- so the bare `%T` is the true statement and is accepted.
fn manufactured(n: %I32) -> %I32 = n + 1

fn main() -> Int:
    let &v = 11 :: I32
    let &row = [1, 2, 3] :: [I32 *3]

    hands():
        v = v + 1

    handsParameter([5, 6]):
        v = v + 1

    return *leaks() + *leaksElement() + *rooted(row) + *manufactured(values(row))
